      "text": "## Why Do Two Sexes Have Different Strategies?"
    },
    {
      "id": "07-SEXUAL-SELECTION.md#006",
      "path": [
        "07-SEXUAL-SELECTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:evolutionary-biology:07-sexual-selection, git-history:evolutionary-biology:07-sexual-selection]",
        "canonical_path": "evolutionary-biology/07-SEXUAL-SELECTION.md",
        "concepts": "[sexual, selection]",
        "current_path": "evolutionary-biology/07-SEXUAL-SELECTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:evolutionary-biology:sexual-selection",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "evolutionary-biology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[sexual, selection]",
        "section": "evolutionary-biology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Sexual Selection and Life History Theory",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Anisogamy**: the fundamental asymmetry. Eggs are large and costly; sperm are\nsmall and cheap."
    },
    {
      "id": "07-SEXUAL-SELECTION.md#007",
      "path": [
        "07-SEXUAL-SELECTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:evolutionary-biology:07-sexual-selection, git-history:evolutionary-biology:07-sexual-selection]",
        "canonical_path": "evolutionary-biology/07-SEXUAL-SELECTION.md",
        "concepts": "[sexual, selection]",
        "current_path": "evolutionary-biology/07-SEXUAL-SELECTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:evolutionary-biology:sexual-selection",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "evolutionary-biology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[sexual, selection]",
        "section": "evolutionary-biology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Sexual Selection and Life History Theory",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n  Parental investment (Trivers 1972):\n  ───────────────────────────────────\n  Sex that invests more per offspring = the \"limited resource\"\n  → Subject to mate choice by the less-investing sex\n  → Typically, but not always: females invest more → males compete for females"
    },
    {
      "id": "07-SEXUAL-SELECTION.md#008",
      "path": [
        "07-SEXUAL-SELECTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:evolutionary-biology:07-sexual-selection, git-history:evolutionary-biology:07-sexual-selection]",
        "canonical_path": "evolutionary-biology/07-SEXUAL-SELECTION.md",
        "concepts": "[sexual, selection]",
        "current_path": "evolutionary-biology/07-SEXUAL-SELECTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:evolutionary-biology:sexual-selection",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "evolutionary-biology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[sexual, selection]",
        "section": "evolutionary-biology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Sexual Selection and Life History Theory",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Bateman's principle (fruit flies):\n  - Male reproductive success limited by number of matings\n  - Female reproductive success limited by number of eggs / resources\n  → Males benefit more from additional matings\n  → This asymmetry drives the evolution of male ornamentation + female choosiness"
    },
    {
      "id": "07-SEXUAL-SELECTION.md#009",
      "path": [
        "07-SEXUAL-SELECTION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:evolutionary-biology:07-sexual-selection, git-history:evolutionary-biology:07-sexual-selection]",
        "canonical_path": "evolutionary-biology/07-SEXUAL-SELECTION.md",
        "concepts": "[sexual, selection]",
        "current_path": "evolutionary-biology/07-SEXUAL-SELECTION.md",
        "id": "maxim:evolutionary-biology:sexual-selection",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "evolutionary-biology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[sexual, selection]",
        "section": "evolutionary-biology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Sexual Selection and Life History Theory",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Caveats:\n  ─────────\n  Sex roles can reverse:\n  - Pipefish, sea horses: males brood → more invested → females compete for males\n  - Jacanas: females hold territories; males incubate; female size > male\n  - Degree of parental investment, not chromosomal sex, predicts which sex\n    is more choosy\n```"
    },
    {
